#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Check the Tier C approval vectors against tier-c-approval/v1 and the contracts it binds.

Three corpora replay one Tier C proposal through replies, commits, dispatches,
outcome writes, crashes, restarts, binding and authority changes,
reconciliations and later proposals, all through the one model
tier-c-approval/v1 states:

- `tier-c-approval/vectors/admission.json`: admission, dispatch, restart,
  reconciliation and the safe default;
- `offline-tokens/vectors/tier-c-binding-v2.json`: the offline-tokens/v2 binding,
  and a v1 token parsed as v1 and refused as Tier C authority;
- `operator-socket/vectors/tier-c-reconcile.json`: `evidence reconcile-tier-c`,
  the authenticated local operator reconciliation in operator-socket/v1.

A fourth, `offline-tokens/vectors/signing-domain-v2.json`, carries real Ed25519
signatures under a published test key, and each is verified here: a v1
verifier over the unprefixed preimage, and a v2 verifier that selects the
domain it reads from offline-tokens/v2 by the exact `token_version` before
verifying, with no trial verification and no fallback.

The model:

- every proposal binds, among the fields tier-c-approval/v1 names, the
  commissioned zone it commands;
- a reply becomes an approval only on its durable commit, and only while the
  commissioned binding and authority snapshot byte-match the proposal's;
- expiry is decided against the creating process's monotonic deadline, never
  the wall clock, and an open proposal does not survive a restart;
- dispatch begins in the same live attempt and waits on nothing;
- the approval commit reserves a terminal uncertainty record: an outcome that
  cannot be appended, a timeout, a lost response, a partial or indeterminate
  result, or a dispatch that finds the pending-outcome ceiling reached turns
  that record into `dispatch_outcome_unknown` with no delay and no replay, and
  only an executor that affirms the commanded outcome did not occur yields
  `dispatch_failed`;
- a contention refusal applies the safe default and never retries, and neither
  it nor a failure replays;
- after a restart nothing is replayed: a durable dispatch-start marker without
  an outcome is `dispatch_outcome_unknown`, an approval is
  `approval_aborted_undispatched` only on affirmative durable proof that
  dispatch was never invoked, and every other approval without an outcome is
  `dispatch_not_proven`;
- closing a proposal `proposal_aborted_restart` or `approval_binding_changed`
  applies the non-actuating safe default;
- a proposal creates at most one durable safe-default intent, keyed by its
  `proposal_id`: an alert is retried through the outbox at least once, each
  attempt carrying the identifier, and may be duplicated after an ambiguous
  provider result, local logging is idempotent by the identifier, and no
  restart, repeated reply or later refusal creates a second intent;
- a proposal row that cannot be committed is never sent, approves nothing and
  claims no durable safe default, and Tier D is unaffected;
- an outcome the live runtime cannot append is `pending`, or `unknown_live` at
  the ceiling, and never a durable `dispatch_outcome_unknown` until written;
  after restart it is `dispatch_outcome_unknown` with a durable marker and
  `dispatch_not_proven` without one;
- a safe default is admitted only at Tier A, classified informational by the
  guard, with a registry entry whose consequence class is `informational` and
  that is `safe_default_eligible` and not `physical`; an action with no
  registry entry is refused, and the pinned actuating actions are each refused;
- a Tier C action able to change physical state is governed only with a
  commissioned zone and outcome, a generic `coap_command` is refused as a Tier
  C proposal, and a host-state Tier C action or a Tier B action requiring
  approval is outside the contract, neither governed nor refused by it;
- the authority snapshot is one closed canonical object whose exact bytes the
  proposal binds;
- an uncertain dispatch blocks only a later Tier C proposal for the same
  outcome on the same commissioned zone, at its creation and again when a
  reply to an already-open proposal is admitted, inside the commit, where an
  approval of the same outcome and zone still in flight also blocks it, which
  then closes `proposal_blocked_uncertain_outcome` with the safe default; it
  is reconciled only by the authenticated local command or commissioned
  feedback, appended and never rewriting the uncertainty;
- the command is submitted to the running runtime over its local operator
  socket, and the bridge opens no store and creates no file, and trusts an
  answer only from a peer it verified as the runtime service identity,
  whatever `--socket` names, reporting any other `runtime_unavailable`; a
  request naming `operation` twice is `invalid_arguments`; the runtime
  admits only root or the installed operator identity from the connection's
  kernel peer credentials by effective user ID alone, never from a request
  field, reads that identity only from a trusted `operator-uid` file, needs no
  store permission from the caller, refuses an unauthorized peer before any
  state and a request naming a member twice, records the audit login user ID
  only for a peer it pinned, records the entry point `local_operator_socket`,
  compares `--device-id` with its own device, audits each other
  principal's identical repeat, owns cancellation and idempotency, and commits
  nothing on an operational outcome; its reasons, refusal codes and their
  order, and its note bound and excluded code points are read from
  operator-socket/v1;
- a v2 offline token approves only the proposal, device, action, target and
  zone it names, never through a wildcard, never beyond the proposal's
  deadline, and its `token_id` is consumed only with a durable approval; a
  payload naming a member twice is malformed; a v1 token
  is never Tier C approval, including for a host-state Tier C action on the
  existing workflow, which no token of either version approves and whose
  token is not consumed; `token_version` is an integer only by its number
  token as written, and the preimage is signing/v2 Profile 2, which escapes
  only `"`, backslash and U+0000-U+001F;
- `approval_timeout_seconds` lies between the schema minimum and the release
  maximum (runtime-config/v2).

Every sentence the model hard-codes is held word for word, the
`decision_state` vocabulary is read from the text and every state in it must be
reached, and `action_records` is read from runtime-health/v3. gateway-api/v1
must carry its pointer to tier-c-approval/v1, and neither it nor
gateway-evidence-carriage/v1 may carry the Tier C vocabulary that contract
defines.

events/v1 and offline-tokens/v1 are held to their published approval semantics:
each must keep its v1 sentences and carry none of the terms v2 introduces. That
text guard proves only that the published v1 prose was not edited and that
proposed v2 vocabulary did not enter v1. It does not prove wire compatibility
or consumer behavior; before events/v2 ratification or implementation, a data
vector and independent v1/v2 consumer checks are mandatory.

    python3 scripts/check-tier-c-approval-vectors
    python3 scripts/check-tier-c-approval-vectors --mutations

`--mutations` corrupts every expectation, drops each required sequence, flips
each configuration and safe-default case, re-signs and re-types tokens, rewords
each hard-coded sentence, removes each state from the vocabulary, removes each
reason and refusal code, swaps each adjacent pair in the refusal order, changes
the note bound and the signature domain, writes v2 terms into each v1 contract,
and switches on model defects a conforming runtime must not have, and requires
every mutant to be refused.
"""

